00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.950 align:start position:0%
in the far north of iraq sidhu province  an ancient mesopotamian town now called  ahmadiyya  lies 1400 meters above sea level  the climate and soil conditions in the  area are good for growing crops  especially sesame  almadia has been famous for its tahini  for centuries  with a staple food paste produced by  traditional methods  suleiman hussein's factory has been in  the family for generations  he says it operates seven months a year  between october and june  that's the rainy season when there's  enough water  and the currents are strong enough to  turn the water mills  the factory water mill was constructed  350 years ago and it's still producing  the same tasty tahini because we use  locally grown sesame soaked in well  water and roasted by firewood  after they're dried the seeds are put in  a stone oven heated by firewood  then follows a labor-intensive stirring  process  for a couple of hours to ensure they're  completely roasted  a water mill driven grinder then turns  them into a smooth paste  the grinder's millstone is turned by  hydropower from underneath the water  cools off the millstone's temperature so  that any seeds won't be burned  workers in this factory say they produce  between 70 and 80 tons of tahini a year  tahini is a favorite food  throughout the arab region  but for our maria families traditional  tahini is not only part of their diet  to face the cold weather in the  mountains  it has also become part of their  heritage  eighty-year-old hasan al-ahmadi says  they've always depended on what they can  grow in the area for their nutritional  needs as food security is a daily  struggle  our founding fathers created this dish  to help them move around heights and  valleys to work in farms as a child i  remember we used it as a dietary  supplement  the people of ahmadiyya say proudly that  customers from across iraq visit the  town just to buy its tahini it's  believed the first tahini water mill was  built by kurdish rulers in the 16th  century  what's not known is how long traditional  mills will keep running without the  introduction of some modern technology  al-jazeera in ahmadiyya town northern  iraq
 

